import exceptions.CurrentlyUnavailableException;

public class USPS implements ShippingStrategy {
    private double baseCost = 5.00;
    private double perKgCost = 0.50;

    @Override
    public void calculateShippingCost(double weight, boolean isInternational) {
        double totalCost = baseCost + (perKgCost*weight);
        System.out.printf("Your total cost for the product to be delivered is : %.2f\n", totalCost);
        return;
    }
    
    @Override
    public void calculateShippingTime(boolean isInternational) {
        try {
            if(isInternational == false) System.out.println("The item will be delivered in 5-7 business days");
        }
        catch(CurrentlyUnavailableException ex) {
            if(isInternational) {
                throw new CurrentlyUnavailableException("Sorry, Internation Delivery Is Not Available Currently!");
            }
        }
        catch(Exception ex) {
            throw new Exception("Unknown Exception has occured");
        }
    }
}

// Mastering an LLD (Low-Level Design) or Machine Coding round requires shifting your mindset from "How do I write code that works?" to "How do I write code that can survive change, scale, and multiple developers working on it?"Interviewers in machine coding rounds are evaluating your code quality, modularity, adherence to SOLID principles, error handling, and extensibility, not just whether the final output prints to the console.Here is a comprehensive, end-to-end blueprint of what to do, what to code, and to what extent during an LLD or machine coding interview.Phase 1: Clarification & Requirements (First 5–10 Minutes)Never start coding immediately. If you code without clarifying, you will build the wrong thing.What to do: Ask scope-defining questions. Divide requirements into Functional (What the system must do) and Non-Functional (Concurrency, extensibility, performance).To what extent:Do not try to build every feature mentioned in the prompt. Pick the Core MVP (Minimum Viable Product) requirements and state: "I will focus on implementing Core Features X and Y first, and if time permits, I will add Z."Clarify scale (e.g., “Should this handle multi-threading / concurrent users, or is a single-threaded CLI sufficient for now?”).Phase 2: Core Entities & Class Design (Minutes 10–20)Before opening your IDE or writing a single line of logic, map out your domain model.What to do: Identify the nouns in the problem statement (these become your classes/models) and the verbs (these become your methods/services).What to output (Mentally or on a scratchpad):Domain Models / Entities: (e.g., User, Order, Product, Payment). Keep these as clean Plain Old Java Objects (POJOs) or data classes with getters, setters, and constructors.Enums: Use enums for fixed states (e.g., OrderStatus: PENDING, PROCESSING, SHIPPED, CANCELLED).Interfaces: Think about what behaviors will have multiple variations (e.g., ShippingStrategy, PaymentGateway, NotificationService). This is where design patterns emerge naturally.Phase 3: Applying Design Patterns & SOLID (The Architecture)What to do: Apply design patterns only where they solve a clear problem, never force-fit them just to show off.Key Patterns to Master for LLD:Strategy Pattern: When you have interchangeable algorithms (like our shipping carriers or payment gateways).Factory Pattern: When object creation logic is complex or needs to be hidden from the client (e.g., creating the right payment processor based on a string).Observer Pattern: For event-driven notifications (e.g., when an order status changes, notify email service, inventory service, and analytics service).Singleton Pattern: For shared resources like configuration managers or database connection pools (use sparingly).To what extent: Ensure your classes obey SOLID principles—especially the Single Responsibility Principle (e.g., don't put database queries inside your business logic classes if you can separate them into repositories/services) and the Open/Closed Principle (adding a new feature should require writing a new class, not modifying existing classes).Phase 4: Writing the Code (Minutes 20–75) — What to Code & ExtentYou only have 60–90 minutes total, so you must write modular, production-grade skeleton code, not giant files of spaghetti logic.1. Directory / Package Structure (Even in a single file or simple project, think in layers):models/ or entities/ -> Data holders (User, Item)strategies/ or services/ -> Business logic and interface contractsexceptions/ -> Custom business exceptionsenums/ -> Constant states2. Business Logic Extent:Focus on the happy path first, making sure it executes cleanly from end to end.Once the happy path works, layer in the core business validations (e.g., checking if stock is sufficient before placing an order).3. Error and Exception Handling (Crucial for a "Hire" rating):Do NOT use generic try-catch (Exception e) everywhere.DO define 1–2 Custom Unchecked Exceptions (e.g., InsufficientStockException, PaymentFailedException, ResourceNotFoundException).Throw these exceptions inside business rules (using if-else), and catch them gracefully at the entry point/driver layer to print a clean error message rather than crashing with a raw stack trace.Phase 5: Testing & Main Driver (Final 10–15 Minutes)Interviewers want to see that your code actually runs and fulfills the user requirements.What to code:Write a robust Main class (or Driver class) that simulates a real-world flow.Example flow: Initialize services $\rightarrow$ create a user $\rightarrow$ add items to cart $\rightarrow$ execute checkout using a specific strategy $\rightarrow$ handle an edge case (like triggering an exception).To what extent: You don't need a full JUnit test suite unless explicitly requested, but having a clean main method that demonstrates polymorphism, exception handling, and core workflows in action is mandatory.📋 Summary Checklist: What Interviewers Look ForComponentWhat to Do / ExtentUML / Class DiagramQuick mental sketch or text outline. Focus on relationships (Composition vs. Inheritance).InterfacesDefine contracts for behavior (e.g., PaymentService, PricingStrategy).EncapsulationKeep entity fields private. Use getters/setters. Avoid public mutable state everywhere.Error HandlingCustom runtime exceptions for business rule violations (InvalidCartException).Concurrency / Thread SafetyMention thread-safety if relevant (e.g., using ConcurrentHashMap for in-memory datastores, or synchronized blocks if multiple threads access inventory).Code CleanlinessMeaningful variable names, small methods (Single Responsibility per method), no dead code.